## Expert cache

![expert cache](../results/fig_expert_cache.png)

We fit a sticky-Zipf trace generator to the published DeepSeek-V2-Lite LRU miss rate: 28% at 32 of 64
experts, matched at {{ pct(1 - D.expert_cache.locality_fit.lru_hit_dsv2lite) }} with stay = {{ D.expert_cache.locality_fit.stay }}.
Applied to V4.1's 384 experts, with each per-chip expert slice {{ D.expert_cache.per_slot_mb|n(1) }} MB:

| slots/layer | SRAM per chip | LRU | LFU | Belady OPT |
|---|---|---|---|---|
{% for i in range(D.expert_cache.curves.lru|length) %}
| {{ D.expert_cache.curves.lru[i].slots }} | {{ D.expert_cache.curves.lru[i].sram_gb_per_chip|n(1) }} GB | {{ D.expert_cache.curves.lru[i].hit|pct }} | {{ D.expert_cache.curves.lfu[i].hit|pct }} | {{ D.expert_cache.curves.opt[i].hit|pct }} |
{% endfor %}

**Negative result.** An expert cache that would matter needs gigabytes per chip, and SRAM offers
hundreds of megabytes. Hit rate would pay handsomely ({{ D.expert_cache.gains[3].tok_s_user|n }} tok/s/user at 40%) if it
could be had. The traces are synthetic; real V4.1 routing traces would settle it.

